  "$comment": "Threshold rules: a predicate plus a bound, evaluated by filtering the document and counting the matches. go/compliance_test.go and test/compliance.test.ts read THIS file and run THESE cases, so the two evaluators are held to one contract. 'now' is the injected reference clock, so POA&M and override expiry are properties of the fixture rather than of the day the suite runs. The fixture deliberately includes a requirement whose governing waiver moves it off 'failed', because the point of keying rules on status is that amendments are already applied when a rule sees the document. Note the passing count is TWO, not one: WAIVED-CRITICAL's governing waiver sets its status to passed, which is the whole reason rules key on effective status. refusalByCount pins the wording and the singular/plural of the message a grid-only evaluation emits for a rules-bearing spec; both languages assert against it rather than against their own copy. predicateFields pins the predicate surface in one place. Go maps predicate fields onto the filter explicitly while TypeScript spreads ...where, so a field added to one language reaches the filter there and silently does nothing in the other; both suites assert their own definition against this list, so adding a field means updating all three or a test goes red. FINDINGS: each case also pins expectFindings — one list per expected violation, in the same order as expect, naming the requirements that breached that bound by id, title, status and severity. Only the MESSAGE half was pinned here before, so the findings a violation carries were asserted by two independently hand-written suites that could diverge silently; they did, and the requirement TITLE ended up unasserted in BOTH languages at once, which is what this section exists to prevent. The title is included deliberately rather than ids alone: it is the field that was missing and the one the guide's console blocks promise. GRID CASES: gridCases pins the same contract for the count/compliance/controls bounds, which resolve their offenders from the control map rather than from a filter. The two that deliberately name NOTHING are pinned against this same fixture, which DOES produce a non-empty list for the count bound directly above them — so an empty expectation here is a real assertion rather than a vacuous one.",
